122
00:08:03,000 --> 00:08:04,500
Don asked this question.

123
00:08:05,000 --> 00:08:10,500
He says: is it legal for a troop to place in their bylaws

124
00:08:11,000 --> 00:08:16,500
a requirement that a scout must attend so many meetings or outings

125
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:20,500
to be able to advance in rank or hold office?

126
00:08:21,500 --> 00:08:27,000
If they don't wish to be scouts or to accomplish the tasks of scouting,

127
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:31,000
who are we to say that they should attend 70 or 80% of all meetings and events?

128
00:08:31,500 --> 00:08:35,000
I would think that we would want to have scouts coming and attending

129
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:39,000
because they want to, not because we have bylaws saying that they have to.

130
00:08:39,500 --> 00:08:45,000
Well, Don, you know this is a pretty common question

131
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:48,000
and a pretty common mistake that scout leaders make,

132
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:50,500
And I'm going to answer your question and then we're going to talk

133
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,500
a little bit about the broad underlying concepts that affect the answer.

134
00:08:56,000 --> 00:08:59,500
So, first off, the simple answer is no.

135
00:09:00,000 --> 00:09:02,500
Scout units may not do this,

136
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:05,500
Whether it's a troop, a pack or a crew.

137
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:10,500
they cannot set up anything that changes the advancement guidelines.

138
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:12,500
They can't do that.

139
00:09:13,000 --> 00:09:15,500
So let's find out where it says that.

140
00:09:15,500 --> 00:09:21,000
The first place we're going to look is the Advancement Committee Guidebook

141
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:25,000
And there will be a link to a PDF copy of that guidebook

142
00:09:25,500 --> 00:09:32,000
in the blog post that contains this podcast at scoutmastertypepadcom.

143
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:37,000
Here's the active statement in the book.

144
00:09:37,500 --> 00:09:39,000
You'll find it on page three

145
00:09:39,500 --> 00:09:43,500
And it says: No council, district, unit or individual

146
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:51,000
has the authority to add to or subtract from advancement requirements.

147
00:09:51,500 --> 00:09:54,000
So we're talking about the advancement requirement that says:

148
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:59,000
be active in your troop or patrol for you know X number of months

149
00:09:59,500 --> 00:10:02,000
And that's for first class, star life and eagle.

150
00:10:02,500 --> 00:10:09,000
So, and that's for star life and eagle,

151
00:10:09,500 --> 00:10:13,000
The tenure requirement for star life and eagle, about being active.

152
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:23,000
The other place we're going to look is in the 2011 requirements book

153
00:10:23,500 --> 00:10:29,000
And there's a statement that is printed in the 2011 requirements book

154
00:10:29,500 --> 00:10:33,000
And it's the definition of what active means

155
00:10:33,500 --> 00:10:37,000
And a scout will be considered. I'm going to read you exactly what it says:

156
00:10:37,500 --> 00:10:41,000
A scout will be considered active in his unit if he is

157
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:45,500
one registered in his unit and his registration fees are current.

158
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:50,500
Two, he's not dismissed from his unit for disciplinary reasons.

159
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:55,500
Three, he's engaged by his unit leadership on a regular basis

160
00:10:56,000 --> 00:10:58,500
And that means he's informed of unit activities

161
00:10:59,000 --> 00:11:02,500
through a scoutmaster conference or personal contact.

162
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:09,500
And number four, if he's in communication with the unit leader on a quarterly basis,

163
00:11:10,500 --> 00:11:15,000
And it goes on to say this very definitive statement:

164
00:11:15,500 --> 00:11:19,000
Units may not create their own definitions of active.

165
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,000
This is a national standard.

166
00:11:22,500 --> 00:11:25,000
So there you have your answer.

167
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:30,000
We do not set metrics on what active means,

168
00:11:30,500 --> 00:11:32,000
And this isn't my opinion.

169
00:11:32,500 --> 00:11:35,000
This isn't, you know, something that I came up with

170
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:40,500
through some kind of convoluted interpretation of things that I have read.

171
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,500
No, It's right there, It's in black and white.

172
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,500
It doesn't happen, You're not allowed to do it,

173
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:48,500
And if you're doing it, you need to stop.

174
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,500
So let's talk about the broad underlying concepts

175
00:11:52,000 --> 00:11:55,500
about why in the world we would have this kind of policy.

176
00:11:56,000 --> 00:11:59,500
You know I can sympathize with the frustration that would cause somebody to do this,

177
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:01,500
because I've been there

178
00:12:02,000 --> 00:12:04,500
Years and years ago we gave this a shot

179
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,500
And it didn't last very long- And it's good that it didn't-

180
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:11,500
And we got rid of all this crazy bylaws, business

181
00:12:12,000 --> 00:12:14,500
and leadership contracts and stuff like that.

182
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:16,500
Now here's what happens.

183
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:20,500
So there'll be a series of scout meetings and outings.

184
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,500
You know this might be over several months

185
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:26,500
And the attendance won't be all that great.

186
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,500
It'll be very, very soft

187
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:32,500
And the scouts won't seem to care.

188
00:12:32,500 --> 00:12:35,000
You'll get aggravated because you put a lot of time and effort

189
00:12:35,500 --> 00:12:37,000
into your volunteer work as a scouter.

190
00:12:37,500 --> 00:12:41,000
And so you will turn to the tools that seem to be

191
00:12:41,500 --> 00:12:43,000
the most effective and available,

192
00:12:43,500 --> 00:12:46,000
which are pieces of paper with words on them.

193
00:12:46,500 --> 00:12:49,000
This is almost totally meaningless to you

194
00:12:49,500 --> 00:12:52,000
if you're 12,, 13,, 14,, 15 years old.

195
00:12:52,500 --> 00:12:57,000
You, as a scout leader, will go ahead and write your 70% rule,

196
00:12:57,500 --> 00:13:01,000
your 80% rule, and you'll take it to the troop committee

197
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,500
and have them memorialize it in a set of bylaws

198
00:13:04,000 --> 00:13:05,500
or something like that.

199
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:09,500
And then you know, or you'll write leadership contracts

200
00:13:10,000 --> 00:13:11,500
because you'll sense, hey, the patrol leaders

201
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:13,500
really aren't doing their job,

202
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:15,500
They're really not responsible,

203
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,500
They're not being mature.

204
00:13:18,000 --> 00:13:20,500
And so you'll sit down and draft a contract

205
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,500
and the committee will look at it and pat you on the back

206
00:13:24,000 --> 00:13:25,500
and you know you'll get it all set up.

207
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,500
It'll all be nice and tight and legal and everything.

208
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:30,500
And then you'll put it in front of the boys

209
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:32,500
and they'll all stare at it like a dog with a new pan

210
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,500
and wonder what in the world that it is.

211
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:38,500
Here's what we think is going to happen.

212
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,500
You know, we're going to go through all these machinations.

213
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:43,500
We're going to create our bylaws,

214
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:45,500
We're going to create our rules and our contracts and things,

215
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:48,500
And then we're going to sit down with the scouts

216
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:50,500
and we're going to say to a 14-year-old boy:

217
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:52,500
we're going to put a leadership contract in front of them

218
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:54,500
and say, well, here's your leadership contract

219
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:56,500
for being a patrol leader

220
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:58,500
And we need you to sign this

221
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:00,500
because you're going to agree to do all these things

222
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:02,500
And you know that 14-year-old is going to sit there

223
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:04,500
and he's going to go: wow, well, listen,

224
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:06,500
I really appreciate this.

225
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:08,500
I never really thought about this before.

226
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:10,500
I need to be mature and responsible

227
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:12,500
And there's a set of tasks that I need to accomplish,

228
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,500
and I really appreciate you bringing that to my attention.

229
00:14:16,000 --> 00:14:17,500
Here it is. It's in a contract,

230
00:14:18,000 --> 00:14:19,500
So let me go ahead and sign that.

231
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:21,500
And can I have my people look at this?

232
00:14:22,000 --> 00:14:23,500
By the way, before we do this,

233
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:25,500
I just want to make sure everything's legal,

234
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,500
And then their behavior will somehow magically change

235
00:14:28,500 --> 00:14:31,000
or alter and they'll begin attending all the events

236
00:14:31,500 --> 00:14:35,000
and outings and they'll be mature, responsible adults.

237
00:14:35,500 --> 00:14:37,000
And you're going to do this by writing things

238
00:14:37,500 --> 00:14:39,000
on pieces of paper.

239
00:14:39,500 --> 00:14:41,000
Man, it's not going to happen.

240
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:43,000
It's just not going to happen.

241
00:14:43,500 --> 00:14:47,000
I've tried it. I can guarantee it's not going to work.

242
00:14:47,500 --> 00:14:49,000
So what does work?

243
00:14:49,500 --> 00:14:53,000
First, scouts leading their own program

244
00:14:53,500 --> 00:14:55,000
and leading their troop.

245
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:56,500
Not just because they have patches,

246
00:14:57,000 --> 00:15:00,500
not just because you think that you have a youth-led troop.

247
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:02,500
I mean, really go and look at it.

248
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:04,500
Who's doing what?

249
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,500
Who's making the decisions, Who's making the calls,

250
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:09,500
Who's standing up in front at meetings,

251
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:12,500
Who's instructing, Who's leading outings?

252
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:14,500
If it's the youth, you're not going to have

253
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:16,500
an attendance problem. You're really not.

254
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,500
You're going to have isolated difficulties

255
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:23,500
where it's you know, SAT weekend or PSAT weekend

256
00:15:23,500 --> 00:15:27,000
or homecoming or band practice, or you know

257
00:15:27,500 --> 00:15:30,000
some important sports event or something like that.

258
00:15:30,500 --> 00:15:31,000
Well, that's going to happen.

259
00:15:31,500 --> 00:15:33,000
We cooperate with those things.

260
00:15:33,500 --> 00:15:35,000
We don't fight against them.

261
00:15:35,500 --> 00:15:37,000
We cooperate with them.

262
00:15:37,500 --> 00:15:39,000
They're all part of growing up

263
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:41,000
and being a part of an active community

264
00:15:41,500 --> 00:15:42,000
And scouting is all about that.

265
00:15:42,500 --> 00:15:44,000
So we cooperate with those things.

266
00:15:44,500 --> 00:15:49,000
If a bunch of our guys need to be at a homecoming event

267
00:15:49,500 --> 00:15:51,000
or at a PSAT

268
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:52,500
or something like that,

269
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:54,500
oh well, you know that happens.

270
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:56,500
Schedules, conflict-

271
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:58,500
Are you getting the message here?

272
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:02,500
Scouting is not about trying to legislate

273
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:06,500
or lawyer boys into a certain type of behavior.

274
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,500
Scouting is about helping them become aware

275
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:12,500
that these things are desirable,

276
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:16,500
that this is how you become a responsible, mature adult.

277
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:18,500
And if they're leading, they're true.

278
00:16:18,500 --> 00:16:20,000
And if they're leading, they're true

279
00:16:20,500 --> 00:16:23,000
And they have a true sense of responsibility for what happens.

280
00:16:23,500 --> 00:16:26,000
If nobody's bailing them out when they make mistakes,

281
00:16:26,500 --> 00:16:29,000
if nobody's hollering at them and carrying on

282
00:16:29,500 --> 00:16:34,000
and trying to expect them to be a mature, responsible adult,

283
00:16:34,500 --> 00:16:36,000
they're going to show up

284
00:16:36,500 --> 00:16:38,000
And they're going to be interested in what's going on

285
00:16:38,500 --> 00:16:40,000
And they'll be at the meetings

286
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:42,000
And they'll be at the outings.

287
00:16:42,500 --> 00:16:45,000
And this is not an easy thing for you to achieve

288
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:50,500
as a 45,, 50, 55-year-old man or woman,

289
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:54,500
Because we don't remember very much anymore-

290
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:56,500
as much as we'd like to think we do-

291
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:58,500
what it's like to be 13 or 14.

292
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:02,500
And we don't remember that. you know

293
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,500
we didn't have those capacities at that time

294
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:09,500
And that no piece of paper or writing or rule

295
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:11,500
was going to make us have them.

296
00:17:11,500 --> 00:17:18,000
Now you know it's not as if your 15-year-old senior patrol leader

297
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:23,000
has a set of adult capacities.

298
00:17:23,500 --> 00:17:28,000
You know he's mature, reasonable and responsible,

299
00:17:28,500 --> 00:17:31,000
like a 25 or 35-year-old adult

300
00:17:31,500 --> 00:17:37,000
And that he's just making a choice not to use that capacity.

301
00:17:37,500 --> 00:17:40,000
He does not have that capacity.

302
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,500
He's 15. He's a developing human being

303
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:46,500
And our job is to work along with the stages of development

304
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:48,500
in each individual case.

305
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,500
As soon as you make a hard and fast rule like that

306
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:53,500
about anything and scouting,

307
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:55,500
you're going to find an excellent reason

308
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,500
why the rule doesn't apply to some individual.

309
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:04,500
If one of your boys is a national honors society finalist

310
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,500
and has to travel someplace,

311
00:18:06,500 --> 00:18:10,000
if he is a member of the future business leaders of America

312
00:18:10,500 --> 00:18:12,000
or the 4-H or something like that-

313
00:18:12,500 --> 00:18:14,000
and a lot of the scouts get involved in things like that-

314
00:18:14,500 --> 00:18:17,000
if he's a member of a lacrosse or a football or wrestling

315
00:18:17,500 --> 00:18:19,000
or a basketball or a swimming team

316
00:18:19,500 --> 00:18:22,000
and they all of a sudden go to districts

317
00:18:22,500 --> 00:18:24,000
or they go to states or something

318
00:18:24,500 --> 00:18:26,000
you know, you're not going to see that much of him

319
00:18:26,500 --> 00:18:29,000
And that's great because he's doing other things

320
00:18:29,500 --> 00:18:31,000
And the things that he is learning and scouting

321
00:18:31,500 --> 00:18:34,000
is helping to motivate that, for him to be able to do that,

322
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:35,500
And scouting is helping to motivate that

323
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,500
for him to be an active, contributing member

324
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:40,500
of his community.

325
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,500
So we don't need to monopolize

326
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:45,500
all of their time and attention.

327
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:47,500
We just don't.

328
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:49,500
So I hope you know I've been rambling a little bit here,

329
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,500
but I hope that the broad underlying concepts

330
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:54,500
help inform you a little bit about this.

331
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,500
Is it frustrating to deal with? Absolutely.

332
00:18:58,000 --> 00:18:59,500
Is it discouraging at times,

333
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:01,500
Does it just, you know, make your ears turn red

334
00:19:01,500 --> 00:19:03,000
and steam start coming out?

335
00:19:03,500 --> 00:19:05,000
Yep, it'll happen.

336
00:19:05,500 --> 00:19:08,000
But once you have got yourself grounded

337
00:19:08,500 --> 00:19:10,000
in a couple of ideas

338
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:12,000
that you cooperate with your community,

339
00:19:12,500 --> 00:19:14,000
you don't fight with it,

340
00:19:14,500 --> 00:19:16,000
that you want your scouts doing all these wonderful,

341
00:19:16,500 --> 00:19:18,000
great things that help them grow up and mature

342
00:19:18,500 --> 00:19:20,000
and become good human beings.

343
00:19:20,500 --> 00:19:23,000
that means you pay the price every once in a while

344
00:19:23,500 --> 00:19:26,000
of having very few boys at a meeting or an outing.

345
00:19:26,500 --> 00:19:29,000
You're going to get over it,

346
00:19:29,500 --> 00:19:31,000
Don. I hope that helps.

347
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:33,000
I'm going to answer your question.

348
00:19:33,500 --> 00:19:36,000
If you have any more ideas or concepts or questions

349
00:19:36,500 --> 00:19:38,000
about this particular subject, get in touch.

350
00:19:38,500 --> 00:19:40,000
You can do that on the blog,

351
00:19:40,500 --> 00:19:42,000
You can do it on Facebook.

352
00:19:42,500 --> 00:19:44,000
Hey, I'm going to tell you all about that

353
00:19:44,500 --> 00:19:46,000
in a couple of minutes. anyway,

354
00:19:46,500 --> 00:19:48,000
This is Cliff Jacobson

355
00:19:48,500 --> 00:19:50,000
and you're listening to the Scoutmaster Podcast

356
00:19:50,500 --> 00:19:52,000
with Clark Green

357
00:20:01,500 --> 00:20:03,000
Scout.

358
00:20:32,500 --> 00:20:36,000
He's my favorite all time boy scout.

359
00:20:38,500 --> 00:20:41,000
Don't be afraid, you can call me.

360
00:20:41,500 --> 00:20:45,000
Tell me and I'll be around.

361
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:48,000
Howdy Clark, my name is Todd Drady.

362
00:20:48,500 --> 00:20:52,000
I'm with troop 875 in St Augustine, Florida.

363
00:20:52,500 --> 00:20:54,000
I've got a quick question for you.

364
00:20:54,500 --> 00:20:56,000
You seem to have a problem in our troop

365
00:20:56,500 --> 00:21:00,000
with scouts and adults both wanting to join

366
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:04,500
campouts at the last minute, lack of communication

367
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:08,500
or whatnot being the standard excuse,

368
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:10,500
which may or may not be true,

369
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:14,500
but just wondering how most troops handle signups

370
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:19,500
for campouts, how far out should we be requiring

371
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:23,500
a response and a commitment, and how do we keep

372
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:27,500
both adults and scouts to keep their commitments

373
00:21:27,500 --> 00:21:30,000
up on campouts so that we can do proper planning,

374
00:21:30,500 --> 00:21:34,000
both for meals and for how many campsites to book,

375
00:21:34,500 --> 00:21:37,000
etc. It gets exceedingly difficult when we have

376
00:21:37,500 --> 00:21:40,000
last minute additions, last minute deletions,

377
00:21:40,500 --> 00:21:43,000
things like that. So any advice, we appreciate it.

378
00:21:43,500 --> 00:21:44,000
Thank you,

379
00:21:44,500 --> 00:21:46,000
Hey, Todd. well, thanks for getting in touch.

380
00:21:46,500 --> 00:21:48,000
You can leave a voicemail, just like Todd did,

381
00:21:48,500 --> 00:21:51,000
and you're going to get information on how to do that

382
00:21:51,500 --> 00:21:53,000
here in just a moment.

383
00:21:53,500 --> 00:21:55,000
But let's get to the question

384
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:58,500
Right now. Todd, a lot of people are nodding their heads

385
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,500
and saying, yeah, we have this problem too.

386
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,500
I still have it. I have this problem all the time,

387
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:08,500
And I've got to tell you that I've got a couple of pieces

388
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,500
of advice that you might find more aggravating

389
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:18,500
than enlightening. So forgive me, but the first thing

390
00:22:19,000 --> 00:22:23,500
I'm going to say is: you have a youth-led troop,

391
00:22:23,500 --> 00:22:26,000
And if you have a youth-led troop, they're responsible

392
00:22:26,500 --> 00:22:28,000
for making these arrangements and communicating

393
00:22:28,500 --> 00:22:31,000
and things like that, and it's not going to be seamless,

394
00:22:31,500 --> 00:22:34,000
not by any stretch of the imagination.

395
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:39,000
And if it becomes an ongoing concern, then the first thing

396
00:22:39,500 --> 00:22:42,000
I would do as a scoutmaster would be to go to my

397
00:22:42,500 --> 00:22:45,000
patrol leader's council and ask them the question,

398
00:22:45,500 --> 00:22:48,000
just as you put it to me in your voicemail.

399
00:22:48,500 --> 00:22:51,000
Hey, you know, we have last minute additions

400
00:22:51,000 --> 00:22:54,500
and deletions and that causes us some trouble.

401
00:22:55,000 --> 00:22:58,500
And how do you guys think we might be able to fix that?

402
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:00,500
How can we make this better?

403
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,500
They might have some really brilliant ideas.

404
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,500
To tell you the truth, some of this comes under

405
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,500
not a problem to be solved and fixed and smoothed out,

406
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,500
so much as it's part of the price of doing business

407
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:15,500
as a Boy Scout troop. Here's what happens:

408
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,500
Two weeks ahead of time, the boy makes a firm commitment

409
00:23:18,500 --> 00:23:21,000
to go on the camping trip. Two days ahead of time,

410
00:23:21,500 --> 00:23:23,000
he finds out something really cool is going on

411
00:23:23,500 --> 00:23:25,000
and he decides that he's going to do that

412
00:23:25,500 --> 00:23:27,000
rather than go on the camping trip.

413
00:23:27,500 --> 00:23:29,000
He doesn't tell anybody and you know

414
00:23:29,500 --> 00:23:31,000
you're standing there on a Friday night

415
00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:33,000
wondering where he is and you call him up

416
00:23:33,500 --> 00:23:36,000
and oh, I'm not going and oh, okay,

417
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:39,000
Or the other way around. the boy knows

418
00:23:39,500 --> 00:23:41,000
that the camping trip's coming up

419
00:23:41,500 --> 00:23:43,000
and he thinks, wow, well, wait a minute,

420
00:23:43,500 --> 00:23:45,000
that really cool thing is happening that weekend,

421
00:23:45,500 --> 00:23:47,000
so I'm not going to go. And he goes home

422
00:23:47,000 --> 00:23:48,500
and then, round about Wednesday

423
00:23:49,000 --> 00:23:52,500
of the camping trip weekend, he mentions to his parents

424
00:23:53,000 --> 00:23:54,500
that he's going to go do the really cool thing

425
00:23:55,000 --> 00:23:57,500
during the weekend and the parents say,

426
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,500
yeah, no, you're not actually.

427
00:24:01,000 --> 00:24:03,500
And so he decides that well, I'll go on the scout trip.

428
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:05,500
and then he shows up on Friday

429
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,500
and nobody knows that he was coming.

430
00:24:09,000 --> 00:24:11,500
You know he doesn't have a permission slip,

431
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,500
he didn't pay for the trip. Yeah, it's a giant mess.

432
00:24:14,500 --> 00:24:16,000
It's a giant mess. It's a giant mess

433
00:24:16,500 --> 00:24:18,000
and it's a real problem.

434
00:24:18,500 --> 00:24:20,000
I'm not discounting that. it's, you know,

435
00:24:20,500 --> 00:24:23,000
not a real problem and it's not really aggravating.

436
00:24:23,500 --> 00:24:25,000
I guess what I'm saying is is that

437
00:24:25,500 --> 00:24:27,000
there's not really a good, clean way

438
00:24:27,500 --> 00:24:31,000
to have it done without getting pretty regimented

439
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:35,000
and creating a list of rules and procedures-

440
00:24:35,500 --> 00:24:38,000
And I don't like lists of rules and procedures

441
00:24:38,500 --> 00:24:42,000
because they rarely work, at least in my experience.

442
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:46,500
So my idea is this: I want scouts to go camping.

443
00:24:47,000 --> 00:24:50,500
If they show up late, we'll try and find a way.

444
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:54,500
And I put it squarely on the shoulders of the boys

445
00:24:55,000 --> 00:24:56,500
who are leading their troop.

446
00:24:57,000 --> 00:24:59,500
Hey, John, show it up. What are you guys going to do?

447
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,500
Can we make a way for him to be there?

448
00:25:03,000 --> 00:25:04,500
Will it work out okay,

449
00:25:05,000 --> 00:25:07,500
Or do you need to send him home?

450
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,500
No, They'll usually find a way to make it work.

451
00:25:10,500 --> 00:25:13,000
And it's a hot mess. It's a hot mess all the time.

452
00:25:13,500 --> 00:25:15,000
Patrols show up without food.

453
00:25:15,500 --> 00:25:17,000
Every once in a while that happens.

454
00:25:17,500 --> 00:25:19,000
They think two or three guys are coming

455
00:25:19,500 --> 00:25:21,000
and those guys don't show up.

456
00:25:21,500 --> 00:25:23,000
or they think two or three guys aren't coming

457
00:25:23,500 --> 00:25:25,000
and all of a sudden those guys show up.

458
00:25:25,500 --> 00:25:29,000
And you know, this sounds a little Pollyannish maybe,

459
00:25:29,500 --> 00:25:32,000
but it's an opportunity for your scouts

460
00:25:32,500 --> 00:25:34,000
to start working in the real world

461
00:25:34,500 --> 00:25:37,000
and trying to figure out some of this stuff

462
00:25:37,500 --> 00:25:39,000
and trying to put it together.

463
00:25:39,000 --> 00:25:40,500
Now I know that I will get some responses

464
00:25:41,000 --> 00:25:44,500
from people who have an excellent administrative style

465
00:25:45,000 --> 00:25:48,500
and they have got this kind of wired and figured out

466
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:50,500
and it's a long tradition in their troop

467
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:52,500
and they've got it figured.

468
00:25:53,000 --> 00:25:55,500
We tried legislating this at one point

469
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,500
and having rules and regulations and procedures.

470
00:25:59,000 --> 00:26:00,500
You know, if you weren't current on your dues,

471
00:26:01,000 --> 00:26:02,500
you didn't get to go camping

472
00:26:03,000 --> 00:26:04,500
If you didn't make a commitment at least two weeks ahead of time,

473
00:26:05,000 --> 00:26:06,500
you didn't get to go camping.

474
00:26:06,500 --> 00:26:08,000
It just didn't work. for us

475
00:26:08,500 --> 00:26:09,000
It didn't.

476
00:26:09,500 --> 00:26:11,000
Sometimes it's the family's difficulty,

477
00:26:11,500 --> 00:26:14,000
It's the parents who, you know,

478
00:26:14,500 --> 00:26:16,000
frankly, don't have it together

479
00:26:16,500 --> 00:26:19,000
and they don't make the right arrangements and things.

480
00:26:19,500 --> 00:26:21,000
so a boy ends up not going on a trip

481
00:26:21,500 --> 00:26:24,000
he was supposed to go on or showing up for a trip

482
00:26:24,500 --> 00:26:26,000
that nobody knew he was going on.

483
00:26:26,500 --> 00:26:29,000
And in that situation,

484
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:31,000
when you kind of suss out

485
00:26:31,500 --> 00:26:33,000
that it might be a parental problem,

486
00:26:33,000 --> 00:26:34,500
I try not to penalize the boys

487
00:26:35,000 --> 00:26:37,500
for problems that their parents make,

488
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:39,500
if it's at all possible.

489
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,500
I hope some of this helps you, Todd,

490
00:26:43,000 --> 00:26:45,500
really, because the intention is to be helpful

491
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:47,500
And I would like to hear

492
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:49,500
other people's take on the question.

493
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:51,500
But just to sum up,

494
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,500
I would take this question to your patrol leaders council

495
00:26:55,000 --> 00:26:58,500
see what they have to suggest in the way of resolutions.

496
00:26:59,000 --> 00:27:00,500
I would also realize that

497
00:27:00,500 --> 00:27:02,000
part of this is a price of doing business.

498
00:27:02,500 --> 00:27:05,000
I haven't made it happen seamlessly

499
00:27:05,500 --> 00:27:07,000
in the last 26 years,

500
00:27:07,500 --> 00:27:10,000
So we put up with some uncertainty

501
00:27:10,500 --> 00:27:13,000
and a little bit of chaos once in a while.

502
00:27:13,500 --> 00:27:16,000
But the idea of getting guys out on trips,

503
00:27:16,500 --> 00:27:19,000
that to me seems to be the most important concept

504
00:27:19,500 --> 00:27:22,000
